<section class="py-40 px-6 md:px-8 bg-surface-container-low border-y border-outline-variant/5" id="story">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-8 text-center" data-reveal>
    <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold font-headline leading-none uppercase tracking-tighter">
      STOP <span class="text-primary">CONTEXT</span> SWITCHING.
    </h2>
    <p class="text-on-surface-variant text-lg leading-relaxed">
      You're in the flow. The code is writing itself. Then you need a ticket ID — alt-tab, Chrome,
      forty-seven tabs screaming for attention. You find the ticket, but someone just replied on Slack.
    </p>
    <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-primary pl-6 py-3 italic font-medium bg-primary/5 text-left">
      "The terminal is where developers are most productive. Don't force them out of it."
    </blockquote>
    <p class="text-on-surface-variant text-lg leading-relaxed">
      flowlane started as a side project at <a href="https://saply.ai" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer" class="text-primary underline underline-offset-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-opacity">Saply</a> — one developer's refusal to do the browser dance,
      grown into a tool used every day. It's for the developer who lives on the home row, and for the
      team on Azure DevOps, GitHub, or Jira that wants tickets, branches, and PRs handled without
      switching tools.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>